#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "979859e4-b60c-548d-8da6-b180400e1b07" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "14e05d4f-373c-5d1f-aa59-7ee52466480e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14762" :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\det(A) &= 0\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ -12 & 1\\end{vmatrix}- (-3)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+ 6\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{vmatrix} \\\\[0.6em]\n&= 0(8 + 96) + 3(8 + 48) + 6(-96 + 48) \\\\\n&= 0 + 3 \\cdot 56 + 6 \\cdot (-48) \\\\\n&= 168 - 288 \\\\\n&=-120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059376 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "d7516cec-bd96-59db-98cd-5d82e41fa719" :question/math-academy-id "e-14763" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\n1. $\\det(A)=0$.\n2. $A$ is **not** a singular matrix.\n3. $A$ is a singular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0$.\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\det(A) &= 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}- 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+ 3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\[0.6em]\n&= 1(0 - 4) - 2(7 - 3) + 3(4 - 0) \\\\\n&=-4 - 2 \\cdot 4 + 3 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&=-4 - 8 + 12 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=0$, the matrix is singular.\n\nTherefore, only statements 1 and 3 are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "7780340c-43d6-5d38-9270-865914098218" :question/math-academy-id "e-14764" :question/problem "Given that $\\det(A)=2$, find the value of the constant $k$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ k & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\det(A) &= 3\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}- 1\\begin{vmatrix}k & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+ 2\\begin{vmatrix}k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\[0.6em]\n&= 3(12 - 10) - 1(3k - 0) + 2(2k - 0) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2 - 1 \\cdot 3k + 2 \\cdot 2k \\\\\n&= 6 - 3k + 4k \\\\\n&= k + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $2$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk + 6 &= 2 \\\\\nk &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
